    "q-80663": {
      "dom_id": "step-q80663",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "Consider the linear transformation \n$T:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}3x+2y \\\\ -9x-6y\\end{aligned}]$ \nand the vectors \n${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],{v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}].$\nWhich of the given vectors belong to the kernel of $T?$",
      "worked_solution": "A vector $u$ belongs to the kernel of a linear transformation $T$ if $T(u)=0.$ \n\nTherefore, we need to compute $T({v}_{i})$ for each vector ${v}_{i}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T({v}_{1}) & =T([\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T({v}_{2}) & =T([\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T({v}_{3}) & =T([\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\,T({v}_{1})=T({v}_{3})=0,$ we conclude that the only vectors that belong to the kernel of $\\,T$ are $\\,{v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{3}.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
