    "q-81387": {
      "dom_id": "step-q81387",
      "name": "Question 9",
      "problem": "Consider the linear transformation $T:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}-3x+6y \\\\ 2x-4y\\end{aligned}]$ and the vector $\\,u=[\\begin{aligned}k \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$ Find the value of $\\,k\\,$ such that $\\,u\\in \\text{Ker}(T).$",
      "worked_solution": "A vector $u$ belongs to the kernel of a linear transformation $T$ if $T(u)=0.$ \n\nSo, we need to solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(u) & =0 \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-3\\cdot k+6\\cdot 2 \\\\ 2\\cdot k-4\\cdot 2\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-3k+12 \\\\ 2k-8\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-3k+12=0 \\\\ 2k-8=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution to both of these equations is $k=4.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
