    "q-81389": {
      "dom_id": "step-q81389",
      "name": "Question 10",
      "problem": "Consider the linear transformation $T:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}3x+y \\\\ 15x+5y \\\\ -6x-2y\\end{aligned}]$ and the vector $\\,u=[\\begin{aligned}k \\\\ 6 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$ Find the value of $\\,k\\,$ such that $\\,u\\in \\text{Ker}(T).$",
      "worked_solution": "A vector $u$ belongs to the kernel of a linear transformation $T$ if $T(u)=0.$ \n\nSo, we need to solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(u) & =0 \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}3\\cdot k+6 \\\\ 15\\cdot k+5\\cdot 6 \\\\ -6\\cdot k-2\\cdot 6\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}3k+6 \\\\ 15k+30 \\\\ -6k-12\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}3k+6=0 \\\\ 15k+30=0 \\\\ -6k-12=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution to all three of these equations is $k=-2.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
