    "q-38689": {
      "dom_id": "step-q38689",
      "name": "Question 13",
      "problem": "Consider the linear transformation $T:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}x-2y+z \\\\ 6x-3y-3z \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$ Find $\\,\\text{Ker}(T).$",
      "worked_solution": "Finding the kernel of $T$ is equivalent to finding the null space of the standard matrix of $T.$\n\nFror the given linear transformation, the standard matrix is $T=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 6 & -3 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSetting $x=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}],$ we then need to solve the equation $Tx=0.$ First, we find reduce $T$ to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 6 & -3 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-6{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 9 & -9 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{9}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variable is $x$ and $y$, and we have one free variable, $z.$ That means we need to find $x$ and $y$ in terms of $z.$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get $y=z.$ Substituting these into the first equation, we have \n\n$$\nx=2y-z=2z-z=z.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the kernel of the linear transformation $\\,T$ is $\\{[\\begin{aligned}z \\\\ z \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]\\,:\\,z\\in R\\}.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "option": "a",
