    "q-81392": {
      "dom_id": "step-q81392",
      "name": "Question 17",
      "problem": "Given the linear transformation $T:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}2x-z \\\\ x-y+z \\\\ 3z-6x\\end{aligned}],$ find $\\text{nullity}(T).$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we write down the standard matrix of $T:$\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ -6 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\text{nullity}(T)=\\text{nullity}(T)=\\text{dim}(\\text{Null}(T)).$\n\nIn order to find $\\text{dim}(\\text{Null}(T)),$ we reduce $T$ to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ -6 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{1}{2}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & \\frac{3}{2} \\\\ -6 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & \\frac{3}{2} \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has $1$ non-pivot column (the $3$rd one). Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(T)=\\text{dim}(\\text{Null}(T))=1.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
